- Produce release notes in Markdown format for this Perl CPAN
  distribution release.
- Wrap prose at 72 columns.
- Review all supplied release artifacts, including the ChangeLog,
  release status, diffs, file list, and draft release tarball.
- Treat the draft release tarball as authoritative for the final
  implementation.
- Use the release status to identify added, modified, renamed, and
  deleted files.
- Use the diffs to understand what changed from the previous release.
- Use the ChangeLog as a technical accounting of the release, but do
  not simply restate or reorganize its entries.
- Summarize the important user-visible and developer-visible changes
  rather than repeat implementation details from the ChangeLog.
- Identify the major themes of the release and organize the release
  notes around those themes.
- Give particular attention to:
  - new commands, options, roles, files, and capabilities;
  - behavioral changes;
  - build and release workflow changes;
  - performance improvements;
  - dependency or compatibility changes;
  - changes that affect existing projects or require user action.
- Distinguish substantial features and behavioral changes from
  internal refactoring or implementation cleanup.
- Include implementation details only when they help explain a
  user-visible behavior, compatibility consideration, or important
  architectural change.
- Do not use marketing language or exaggerate the significance of a
  change. Write for experienced Perl developers and maintainers.
- Do not invent motivations, benefits, compatibility implications, or
  behavior that are not supported by the supplied artifacts.
- Before reporting a ChangeLog omission or inconsistency, explicitly
  verify that the behavior, file, or change is not already represented
  elsewhere in the ChangeLog, including under a related build-system
  or grouped entry.
- Do not treat differences between .status, .lst, .diffs, and the
  release tarball as inconsistencies unless the difference contradicts
  the documented purpose and scope of those artifacts.
- Note any inconsistencies, omissions, or apparent errors in the
  ChangeLog separately after the proposed release notes; do not fold
  those observations into the release notes themselves.
- If a new substantive file or capability appears in the release
  status or final tarball but is missing or understated in the
  ChangeLog, call that out in the ChangeLog review.
- Preserve relevant command names, option names, Make targets, module
  names, and configuration keys exactly.
- Prefer concise explanations of why a change matters over exhaustive
  lists of files or methods changed.
- End with a single-sentence commit message describing the overall
  theme of the release.
